    "knowledge_point": "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine",
    "problem": "$\\int (3{cos}^{2}⁡x+1-3{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "We can use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{cos}^{2}⁡x+1-3{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int [3({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)+1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
